{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n## Summary\n\n**Overall Assessment**: `BuggyTooltip` implements only the surface syntax of an accessible tooltip by including `role=\"tooltip\"`, while omitting nearly 85% of the functional requirements specified by the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) and WCAG 2.2 AA. The component is entirely mouse-dependent (inoperable via keyboard), lacks programmatic association between the trigger and content (`aria-describedby` / `id`), cannot be dismissed via `Escape`, and disappears immediately if a user attempts to hover over the tooltip content. It represents a textbook anti-pattern: passing a rudimentary static role check while completely failing real screen reader, keyboard, and low-vision users.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nPrior to auditing the component source code, the following high-risk design gaps were predicted based on common custom tooltip implementations:\n1. **Keyboard Operability Gap**: Trigger will bind only to mouse hover (`mouseenter`/`mouseleave`) and fail to bind to focus events (`focus`/`blur`). *(Confirmed)*\n2. **Missing Programmatic Linkage**: Trigger button will lack `aria-describedby` referencing the tooltip container's `id`. *(Confirmed)*\n3. **WCAG 1.4.13 Violations**: Tooltip will lack `Escape` key dismissal and fail the hoverable requirement (unmounting when cursor moves from trigger to tooltip). *(Confirmed)*\n4. **Form Submission Trap**: Native `<button>` element will omit `type=\"button\"`, causing accidental form submission if nested inside a form. *(Confirmed)*\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Tooltip is mouse-only; completely inoperable for keyboard users\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-13`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/BuggyTooltip.jsx#L8-L13)\n  ```jsx\n  <button\n    onMouseEnter={() => setShowTooltip(true)}\n    onMouseLeave={() => setShowTooltip(false)}\n  >\n    {trigger}\n  </button>\n  ```\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, switch access users, screen reader users navigating via the Tab key.\n- **Why this matters**: Keyboard users who navigate to the trigger button never see or trigger the tooltip because `onFocus` and `onBlur` listeners are absent. Sighted keyboard users are completely denied access to the contextual information provided by the tooltip.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A); WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip Pattern.\n- **Fix**: Add `onFocus` and `onBlur` handlers to mirror hover states:\n  ```jsx\n  onFocus={() => setShowTooltip(true)}\n  onBlur={() => setShowTooltip(false)}\n  ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tooltip-keyboard-inoperable\nfingerprint: c4b19a778e23f401\nsource: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-13\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: Revised Section 508 Chapter 5 (502.4 / E205.4 maps to WCAG 2.0 2.1.1)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=HIGH\nevidence: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-13: button element defines only onMouseEnter and onMouseLeave; no onFocus or onBlur handlers exist.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Render BuggyTooltip. 2. Navigate to the button using the Tab key. 3. Observe that showTooltip remains false and no tooltip content renders.\nexpected_behavior: Focusing the trigger button via keyboard Tab must display the tooltip.\nactual_behavior: Tooltip remains hidden on keyboard focus.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Disconnected DOM semantics; missing `aria-describedby` and tooltip `id`\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/BuggyTooltip.jsx#L8-L21)\n  ```jsx\n  <button ...>{trigger}</button>\n  {showTooltip && (\n    <div className=\"tooltip-content\" role=\"tooltip\">\n      {content}\n    </div>\n  )}\n  ```\n- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, TalkBack).\n- **Why this matters**: While the container possesses `role=\"tooltip\"`, it has no `id`, and the `<button>` trigger has no `aria-describedby`. When a screen reader user focuses the button, the accessibility API only exposes the accessible name of the trigger. Assistive technology is given no indication that supplementary description exists, rendering `role=\"tooltip\"` functionally useless.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A); WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip Pattern.\n- **Fix**: Generate a stable unique identifier (using React 18's `useId`) and associate the trigger with the tooltip:\n  ```jsx\n  const tooltipId = useId();\n  // On trigger:\n  aria-describedby={showTooltip ? tooltipId : undefined}\n  // On tooltip:\n  id={tooltipId}\n  ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tooltip-missing-association\nfingerprint: a981d34e62ff1802\nsource: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip\nsection_508_fpc_context: Revised Section 508 Chapter 5 (502.4 / E205.4 maps to WCAG 2.0 1.3.1 & 4.1.2)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21: <button> lacks aria-describedby attribute; <div role=\"tooltip\"> has no id attribute.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Mount BuggyTooltip. 2. Inspect computed accessibility tree for the button and tooltip elements. 3. Observe the absence of accessible description or aria-describedby relation.\nexpected_behavior: Trigger button must reference the tooltip element ID via aria-describedby so assistive technology announces the tooltip content upon focus.\nactual_behavior: Tooltip element is orphaned in the accessibility tree with no link to the trigger.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Fails WCAG 1.4.13: Non-dismissible via Escape key and not pointer-hoverable\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/BuggyTooltip.jsx#L8-L21)\n- **User Group Impacted**: Low vision users (screen magnification users), keyboard users, cognitive accessibility.\n- **Why this matters**:\n  1. **Dismissible**: There is no `onKeyDown` listener for the `Escape` key. If a tooltip appears and obscures underlying content or adjacent UI elements, the user cannot dismiss it without relocating their focus or mouse cursor.\n  2. **Hoverable**: As soon as the pointer leaves the `<button>` element to inspect or magnify the tooltip content, `onMouseLeave` fires on the button, unmounting the tooltip immediately. Users of screen magnifiers who must move the mouse to read the tooltip cannot do so.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus (Level AA).\n- **Fix**:\n  - Implement an `onKeyDown` listener on the button that dismisses the tooltip when `event.key === 'Escape'`.\n  - Maintain tooltip visibility when the pointer moves over the `.tooltip-content` container itself (e.g. by wrapping both in a shared hover container or managing pointer hover state across both trigger and content).\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tooltip-fails-sc1413-dismissible-hoverable\nfingerprint: f72314dc8029ab55\nsource: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus (Level AA)\nsection_508_fpc_context: Not in scope (SC 1.4.13 was introduced in WCAG 2.1; Revised Section 508 references WCAG 2.0)\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: low_vision=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-21: No Escape key handler implemented; tooltip unmounts synchronously when cursor leaves button boundary.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Hover over trigger button to reveal tooltip. 2. Press Escape key (tooltip fails to dismiss). 3. Move cursor from button into tooltip content (tooltip disappears immediately).\nexpected_behavior: Pressing Escape dismisses tooltip without moving pointer/focus; pointer can move over tooltip without content unmounting.\nactual_behavior: Escape key does nothing; moving cursor to tooltip unmounts content.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction / Edge-Case Defect)\n\n#### 1. Native `<button>` missing explicit `type=\"button\"`\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyTooltip.jsx:8`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/BuggyTooltip.jsx#L8)\n- **User Group Impacted**: All users when the component is placed inside an HTML `<form>`.\n- **Why this matters**: In HTML, standard `<button>` tags default to `type=\"submit\"`. If `BuggyTooltip` is used inside a form (such as an info tooltip icon next to a form label), clicking it will trigger form validation or submission unexpectedly.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.2 SC 3.2.2 On Input (Level A); W3C HTML Specification.\n- **Fix**: Explicitly specify `type=\"button\"` on the `<button>` element.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice)\n\n- **Semantic Role Clarification (Label vs. Description)**: If `{trigger}` is an icon without visible text (e.g. `?` or `\u2139`), the tooltip is functioning as an accessible **name** (`aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`), not supplementary description. Ensure consumer usage guidelines distinguish whether this component is a description tooltip (`aria-describedby`) or an icon-naming tooltip (`aria-labelledby`).\n- **Timing & Debounce**: Add a slight delay (e.g., 200\u2013300ms) before opening on hover to prevent \"tooltip storms\" when a user moves the pointer across multiple interactive elements.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps & Unhandled Edge Cases)\n\n1. **Missing `useId` hook / Stable Unique ID**: The component lacks any mechanism to generate a unique HTML ID to bind `aria-describedby` to `id`. Multiple instances on a page will collide without unique identifiers.\n2. **Missing `Escape` key handling**: When open, pressing `Escape` must close the tooltip and consume the event (preventing parent dialogs or modals from closing unintentionally).\n3. **Missing hover continuity**: Lack of mouse event handling on the tooltip content container itself, causing premature unmounting.\n4. **Missing conditional pointer-events**: If tooltip positioning overlaps interactive elements, ensure the tooltip doesn't block clicks when closing.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Total communication breakdown. Because there is no `aria-describedby` linkage, screen readers announce the trigger button without any reference to the tooltip content. The `role=\"tooltip\"` attribute is rendered completely ineffective.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Total access block. Tabbing to the trigger button displays nothing because `onFocus` is absent. Sighted keyboard users never see the tooltip.\n- **Low vision user**: Severe friction. When zooming in (200%+ or using fullscreen magnifiers), tooltips frequently render partially off-screen. Moving the pointer toward the tooltip content to read it immediately closes it. Additionally, if the tooltip obscures adjacent content, it cannot be dismissed via `Escape`.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Inability to dismiss an intrusive popup without moving mouse focus creates disorientation and cognitive strain.\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Missing `aria-describedby` and `id`; screen reader receives no announcement of tooltip text. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Missing `onFocus`/`onBlur`; tooltip is completely inoperable via keyboard. |\n| **Low vision** | **HIGH** | Violates WCAG 1.4.13; content cannot be hovered or dismissed via `Escape`. |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Inability to dismiss content without pointer movement; risk of obscuring other UI. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No motion, animation, transitions, or parallax effects present. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio cues, alerts, or media elements. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **LOW** | Text styling not present in JSX; handled via external CSS classes. |\n\n---\n\n## Realist Check & Severity Calibration\n\n1. *If shipped as-is, what is the realistic worst-case user experience?*\n   - Sighted keyboard users and screen reader users will never be able to access the tooltip content. Any supplementary information, instructions, or clarifications contained in `content` are permanently lost to them.\n2. *Which user groups are impacted?*\n   - All keyboard-only and assistive technology users.\n3. *How quickly could this be detected in production?*\n   - Automated linters like axe-core may only check for valid role values and pass this component because `role=\"tooltip\"` is technically a valid ARIA role. It would likely slip past automated testing and only be caught during manual audit or assistive technology bug reports.\n4. *Severity calibration check*:\n   - Both Critical findings represent complete access blocks (keyboard inoperability and missing AT association). They survive the realist check and are correctly calibrated at **CRITICAL**.\n\n---\n\n## Self-Audit\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH on all findings.\n- **Can the developer refute this with missing context?**: NO. Native event handlers (`onFocus`/`onBlur`), ARIA association attributes (`aria-describedby`/`id`), and keyboard dismissibility (`Escape`) are objective requirements of WCAG 2.2 and WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip Pattern.\n- **Is this a genuine a11y design gap or a stylistic preference?**: GAP. These are core operability and state communication requirements.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\nThe component is rated **REJECT**. The single accessibility feature present (`role=\"tooltip\"`) is superficial and creates a false sense of compliance. The component violates two Level A criteria (WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships) and one Level AA criterion (WCAG 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus).\n\nTo upgrade the verdict to **ACCEPT**, the implementation must be remediated as follows:\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleTooltip = ({ trigger, content }) => {\n  const [showTooltip, setShowTooltip] = useState(false);\n  const tooltipId = useId();\n\n  const handleKeyDown = (e) => {\n    if (e.key === 'Escape' && showTooltip) {\n      setShowTooltip(false);\n      e.stopPropagation();\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      className=\"tooltip-wrapper\"\n      onMouseEnter={() => setShowTooltip(true)}\n      onMouseLeave={() => setShowTooltip(false)}\n    >\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        aria-describedby={showTooltip ? tooltipId : undefined}\n        onFocus={() => setShowTooltip(true)}\n        onBlur={() => setShowTooltip(false)}\n        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}\n      >\n        {trigger}\n      </button>\n\n      {showTooltip && (\n        <div\n          id={tooltipId}\n          className=\"tooltip-content\"\n          role=\"tooltip\"\n          onMouseEnter={() => setShowTooltip(true)}\n          onMouseLeave={() => setShowTooltip(false)}\n        >\n          {content}\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleTooltip;\n```\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. **Tooltip vs. Toggletip / Disclosure**: Does the `content` contain interactive elements (e.g. links or buttons)? \n   - *Note*: If `content` contains interactive elements, the component **must not** be a tooltip. Tooltips cannot receive focus. If interactive content is required, it must be redesigned as a Popover/Disclosure widget with focus management.\n2. **Naming vs. Describing**: Is `{trigger}` ever an icon without its own text? If so, the component should support an optional `asLabel` prop to switch from `aria-describedby` to `aria-labelledby`.\n",
